    keyTakeaways: [
          "Primary reporting tracked by AINE.WS Newsroom (Local Search Desk).",
          "Key focus: This page provides status information on the services that are part of Google Search. Check back here to view ...",
          "Full technical documentation and release notes are available in the official disclosure linked below."
    ],
    contentHtml: `<article><p><strong>MOUNTAIN VIEW, Calif.</strong> Google’s Search Status Dashboard now shows a fresh November 2026 core update in the Ranking feed, marking the latest broad change to the company’s search systems and adding a new data point for site owners watching traffic swings ahead of the holiday shopping period. The dashboard entry says the rollout began on Nov. 10, 2026, and Google’s own incident page says it applies globally and to all languages, with completion expected over about two weeks.</p><h2>What did Google post on the Search Status Dashboard?</h2><p>Google posted a November 2026 core update to its Search Status Dashboard on Nov. 10, 2026. The live incident page says the update began at 10:06 a.m. Pacific and that the release message was logged at 10:07 a.m. Pacific.</p><p>The incident page describes the event as an incident affecting Ranking, and the message states: “Released the November 2026 core update, which applies globally and to all languages. The rollout may take up to 2 weeks to complete.” The dashboard’s Ranking history also lists the November 2026 core update on Nov. 10 with the duration field shown as “Information.”</p><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Google’s dashboard and incident page</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Product</td><td>Ranking</td></tr><tr><td>Incident</td><td>November 2026 core update</td></tr><tr><td>Start time</td><td>Nov. 10, 2026, 10:06 a.m. Pacific</td></tr><tr><td>Most recent message</td><td>Released the November 2026 core update, which applies globally and to all languages</td></tr><tr><td>Rollout window</td><td>Up to 2 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Scope</td><td>Global, all languages</td></tr><tr><td>Status classification</td><td>SERVICE_INFORMATION</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Why does this matter now?</h2><p>This matters because core updates can move rankings across many sites, but Google says most sites do not need to take immediate action. The company’s guidance is to confirm the update has finished rolling out, then wait at least a full week before analyzing Search Console data.</p><p>That advice is important because Google says the effect of changes can take time to show up. On its core update guidance page, the company says some changes can affect Search results in a few days, but other changes may take several months, and if it has been a few months with no effect, the next core update may be the point when changes surface.</p><h2>What does Google say site owners should do?</h2><p>Google says site owners should compare the right dates in Search Console, then review top pages and queries to judge whether a drop is small or large. If the movement is modest, the company says there is no need for drastic action, and it recommends avoiding changes to content that is already performing well.</p><p>For larger drops, Google advises a deeper assessment of the pages that were most affected and a site wide review of whether the content is helpful, reliable, and people first. The company also warns against quick fixes, saying owners should focus on changes that make sense for users and will last over time.</p><h2>How does this compare with Google’s spam update guidance?</h2><p>Google treats spam updates differently from core updates, but the operating logic is similar: wait for the rollout to finish, then measure the effect. On its spam update guidance page, Google says its automated spam systems are always running, and notable improvements are announced as spam updates on the ranking updates list.</p><p>Google’s spam guidance says sites that change after a spam update should review spam policies, and that compliance issues can lead to lower rankings or removal from results. It also says improvements can take months to register as systems learn that a site now complies with the rules.</p><h2>What are the limits of the dashboard data?</h2><p>The dashboard is useful, but it is not a complete diagnosis tool. Google says the Search Status Dashboard shows issues that affect many sites or users and also lists the latest ranking updates relevant to website owners, which means an annotation may relate to a performance shift without proving causation on its own.</p><p>That limitation matters here. The dashboard confirms a rollout, timing, and scope, but it does not explain how any single site will be affected, which pages will move, or whether ranking changes will persist after the rollout window closes.</p><h2>What should readers watch next?</h2><p>The next checkpoint is the completion of the rollout and the follow up data in Search Console. Google’s own guidance says owners should use the post rollout week as the first clean comparison point, then judge whether traffic or position changes are temporary, broad, or tied to specific sections of a site.</p><p>The Search Status Dashboard also keeps a five year history of incidents and updates, giving publishers a running record of Google’s ranking changes from 2021 through 2026. For now, the current record is clear: Google has put the November 2026 core update on the board, marked it global, and given it a two week rollout window.</p></article>`,
    sources: [
      { name: "Local Search Desk", url: "https://status.search.google.com/products/rGHU1u87FJnkP6W2GwMi/history", context: "Primary wire disclosure." }
    ]
